"On the $(1^2,2^4)$-packing edge-coloring of subcubic graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18353)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Title: On the $(1^2,2^4)$-packing edge-coloring of subcubic graphs\nAuthors: Xujun Liu, Gexin Yu ... Gastineau and Togni (2019), as well as Hocquard, Lajou, and Lu\\v{z}ar (2022), have conjectured that every subcubic graph is $(1^2,2^4)$-packing edge-colorable.\nTitle: On the $(1^2,2^4)$-packing edge-coloring of subcubic graphs\nAuthors: Xujun Liu, Gexin Yu\nDate: Wed Feb 28 14:25:18 2024\n\nAn induced matching in a graph $G$ is a matching such that its end vertices also induce a matching. A $(1^{\\ell}, 2^k)$-packing edge-coloring of a graph $G$ is a partition of its edge set into disjoint unions of $\\ell$ matchings and $k$ induced matchings. Gastineau and Togni (2019), as well as Hocquard, Lajou, and Lu\\v{z}ar (2022), have conjectured that every subcubic graph is $(1^2,2^4)$-packing edge-colorable. In this paper, we confirm that their conjecture is true (for connected subcubic graphs with more than $70$ vertices). Our result is sharp due to the existence of subcubic graphs that are not $(1^2,2^3)$-packing edge-colorable.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPacking edge-colorings of subcubic outerplanar graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X26002568)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Packing edge-colorings of subcubic outerplanar graphs ... In this paper, we confirm the conjecture of Hocquard, Lajou, and Lužar for subcubic outerplanar graphs by showing every subcubic outerplanar graph has a (1,{2}^{5})-coloring and a ({1}^{2},{2}^{3})-coloring.\n\n# Packing edge-colorings of subcubic outerplanar graphs\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Sijin Li ^{a} ^{1}, Yifan Li ^{a} ^{1}, Xujun Liu ^{b} ^{2}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.dam.2026.04.033\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a sequence S=({s}_{1},{s}_{2},\\dots ,{s}_{k}) of non-decreasing positive integers, an S-packing edge-coloring (S-coloring) of a graph G is a partition of E\\left(G\\right) into {E}_{1},{E}_{2},\\dots ,{E}_{k} such that the distance between each pair of distinct edges {e}_{1},{e}_{2}\\in {E}_{i}, 1\\le i\\le k, is at least {s}_{i}+1. In particular, a ({1}^{\\ell },{2}^{k})-coloring is a partition of E\\left(G\\right) into \\ell matchings and k induced matchings, and it can be viewed as intermediate colorings between proper and strong edge-colorings. Hocquard, Lajou, and Lužar conjectured that every subcubic planar graph has a (1,{2}^{6})-coloring and a ({1}^{2},{2}^{3})-coloring.\n\nIn this paper, we confirm the conjecture of Hocquard, Lajou, and Lužar for subcubic outerplanar graphs by showing every subcubic outerplanar graph has a (1,{2}^{5})-coloring and a ({1}^{2},{2}^{3})-coloring. Our results are the best possible since we found subcubic outerplanar graphs with no (1,{2}^{4})-coloring and no ({1}^{2},{2}^{2})-coloring respectively. Furthermore, we explore the question “What are the largest positive integer {k}_{1} and {k}_{2} such that every subcubic outerplanar graph is (1,{2}^{4},{k}_{1})-colorable and ({1}^{2},{2}^{2},{k}_{2})-colorable?”. We prove 3\\le {k}_{1}\\le 6 and 3\\le {k}_{2}\\le 4. We also consider the question “What are the largest positive integer {k}_{1}^{\\prime } and {k}_{2}^{\\prime } such that every 2-connected subcubic outerplanar graph is (1,{2}^{3},{k}_{1}^{\\prime })-colorable and ({1}^{2},{2}^{2},{k}_{2}^{\\prime })-colorable?”. We prove {k}_{1}^{\\prime }=2 and 3\\le {k}_{2}^{\\prime }\\le 11.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the packing edge-colorings of subcubic K4-minor free graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X26003434)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Every subcubic ${K}_{4}$-minor free graph has a $(1,{2}^{5})$-packing edge-coloring. ... The second graph was introduced by Li, Li, and Liu [14], which is a outerplanar graph that is not $({1}^{2},{2}^{2})$-packing edge-colorable.\nTheorem 1.1\n\nEvery subcubic ${K}_{4}$-minor free graph has a $(1,{2}^{5})$-packing edge-coloring.\n\nTheorem 1.2\n\nEvery subcubic ${K}_{4}$-minor free graph has a $({1}^{2},{2}^{3})$-packing edge-coloring.\n\nThe bounds given in Theorem 1.1, Theorem 1.2 are best possible in the sense that there exist subcubic ${K}_{4}$-minor free graphs that are not $(1,{2}^{4})$-packing edge-colorable (the left one in Fig. 1) and not $({1}^{2},{2}^{2})$-packing edge-colorable (the right one in Fig. 1). The second graph was introduced by Li, Li, and Liu [14], which is a outerplanar graph that is not $({1}^{2},{2}^{2})$-packing edge-colorable.\n\nThe paper is organized as follows: in Section 2, we establish some structural results about subcubic ${K}_{4}$-minor free graphs. The proofs for Theorem 1.1, Theorem 1.2 are presented in Sections 3 Proof of, 4 Proof of, respectively.\n\nLet $k$ be an integer. We use $k$-vertex (resp. ${k}^{+}$-vertex) to denote a vertex of degree $k$ (resp. at least $k$), and use $k$-cycle to denote a cycle of size $k$. Let $u\\in V\\left(G\\right)$, a neighbor $v$ of $u$ is called a $k$-neighbor (resp. ${k}^{+}$-neighbor) if $v$ has degree $k$ (resp. at least $k$). Let $e$ and ${e}^{\\prime }$ be two distinct edges of $G$. We say that $e$ sees ${e}^{\\prime }$ if they are adjacent or if another edge joins them. Suppose the graph $G$ is partially colored. We say an edge $e$ sees a color $\\alpha $ if $e$ sees an edge with color $\\alpha $. To keep it simple, we will say a graph is $({1}^{l},{2}^{k})$-colorable (or has a $({1}^{l},{2}^{k})$-coloring) if it is $({1}^{l},{2}^{k})$-packing edge-colorable.\n\n----------------------------------------